# Force LF in the working tree for Markdown files regardless of core.autocrlf.
#
# `oclif readme` regenerates the command block between the <!-- commands -->
# and <!-- commandsstop --> tags using the regex `<!-- commands -->(.|\n)*<!--
# commandsstop -->`. In JS regex, `.` excludes ALL line terminators including
# `\r`, so `(.|\n)*` cannot cross a CRLF line ending. On a CRLF README the
# collapse match fails, the old block is never removed, and oclif prepends a
# fresh block above the stale one -> duplicated commands every run. Keeping
# Markdown as LF in the working tree avoids this.
*.md text eol=lf

# Force LF in the working tree for the AEP golden fixtures regardless of
# core.autocrlf.
#
# The plan-builder tests (test/aep/*.test.ts) compare generated artifact content
# byte-for-byte against these fixtures. Generator output is always LF because
# TypeScript normalizes template-literal line endings to LF when it compiles the
# templates in src/aep/templates, independent of those files' on-disk endings.
# With core.autocrlf=true and no eol pinning, checkout rewrites the fixtures to
# CRLF on Windows, so the comparison fails even though the committed bytes match.
# Pinning the fixtures to LF keeps generator output and fixtures in lockstep on
# every platform.
test/fixtures/aep/golden/** text eol=lf
